## 2. The four manifests

Place these in `platform/cert-manager-config/`. Contents are below; apply them in order.

### 2.1 `00-selfsigned-issuer.yaml` — bootstrap only

```yaml
# Bootstrap issuer. Its ONLY job is to sign the CA certificate below.
# Signs certs that sign themselves — used exactly once, then never again.
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata:
  name: selfsigned-bootstrap
spec:
  selfSigned: {}
```

### 2.2 `01-ca-certificate.yaml` — the trust root

```yaml
# Internal trust root for the OnePass cluster. 5-year lifetime.
# cert-manager generates the keypair, the selfsigned issuer signs it,
# result lands in secret 'onepass-internal-ca-secret' in the
# cert-manager namespace.
#
# NOTE: a ClusterIssuer of type 'ca' resolves its secret from the
# namespace cert-manager runs in — so this secret MUST live in
# 'cert-manager', not in 'pass'.
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: Certificate
metadata:
  name: onepass-internal-ca
  namespace: cert-manager
spec:
  isCA: true
  commonName: onepass-internal-ca
  secretName: onepass-internal-ca-secret
  duration: 43800h    # 5 years
  renewBefore: 4380h  # 6 months
  privateKey:
    algorithm: ECDSA
    size: 256
  issuerRef:
    name: selfsigned-bootstrap
    kind: ClusterIssuer
    group: cert-manager.io
```

### 2.3 `02-ca-issuer.yaml` — signs all service certs

```yaml
# The issuer that signs actual service certificates. Cluster-scoped so
# any namespace can request from it. References the CA secret from 01.
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata:
  name: onepass-ca-issuer
spec:
  ca:
    secretName: onepass-internal-ca-secret
```

### 2.4 `03-pass-offerings-cert.yaml` — the service cert

```yaml
# Service certificate for pass-offerings. Produces secret
# 'pass-offerings-tls' (type kubernetes.io/tls) in the 'pass' namespace,
# containing tls.crt, tls.key, ca.crt.
#
# All four SAN forms included: Kubernetes DNS resolves any of them and
# TLS verification fails if the client hostname isn't on the cert.
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: Certificate
metadata:
  name: pass-offerings-tls
  namespace: pass
spec:
  secretName: pass-offerings-tls
  duration: 2160h    # 90 days
  renewBefore: 720h  # 30 days
  privateKey:
    algorithm: RSA
    size: 2048
    rotationPolicy: Always
  dnsNames:
    - pass-offerings
    - pass-offerings.pass
    - pass-offerings.pass.svc
    - pass-offerings.pass.svc.cluster.local
  issuerRef:
    name: onepass-ca-issuer
    kind: ClusterIssuer
    group: cert-manager.io
```

**JVM option:** if `pass-offerings` is Spring Boot / Java, add the block below to the spec so cert-manager emits a PKCS12 keystore directly (saves the app team converting PEM). Create the password secret first:

```bash
kubectl create secret generic pass-offerings-keystore-password \
  -n pass --from-literal=password='<choose-a-password>'
```

```yaml
  keystores:
    pkcs12:
      create: true
      passwordSecretRef:
        name: pass-offerings-keystore-password
        key: password
```

