## 3. Apply order

```bash
kubectl apply -f 00-selfsigned-issuer.yaml
kubectl apply -f 01-ca-certificate.yaml
kubectl apply -f 02-ca-issuer.yaml
kubectl apply -f 03-pass-offerings-cert.yaml
```

**Why order matters:** 02 references the secret that 01 produces; 03 is signed by 02. Applying out of order leaves resources stuck in `READY=False` until the dependency catches up.

---

## 4. Verification

```bash
# CA root minted
kubectl get certificate -n cert-manager onepass-internal-ca
# expect READY=True

# both issuers ready
kubectl get clusterissuer
# selfsigned-bootstrap and onepass-ca-issuer both READY=True

# service cert issued
kubectl get certificate -n pass pass-offerings-tls
# expect READY=True

# the TLS secret exists
kubectl get secret -n pass pass-offerings-tls
# type: kubernetes.io/tls

# confirm SANs are correct (the important check)
kubectl get secret pass-offerings-tls -n pass \
  -o jsonpath='{.data.tls\.crt}' | base64 -d \
  | openssl x509 -noout -text | grep -A1 "Subject Alternative Name"
# must list all four pass-offerings names
```

**If `pass-offerings-tls` is stuck at `READY=False`:**

```bash
kubectl describe certificate pass-offerings-tls -n pass
# read the Events — almost always the CA issuer wasn't ready yet.
# Confirm onepass-internal-ca (§4 first command) is READY=True, then
# cert-manager will retry automatically.
```

**This phase is DONE when** the SAN check lists all four names. The secret now exists and is correct.

